EXAMPLES=[{'num':'4','title':'Temperature Partial Derivatives','question':'For T(x,y)=2x+5y+xy−40, find and interpret Tₓ(9,5), Tᵧ(9,5), with y measured in hundreds of megawatts.','steps':['For Tₓ hold y constant: derivative of 2x is 2 and of xy is y, so Tₓ=2+y.','At (9,5), Tₓ=7. A 1°C increase in incoming-water temperature produces a 7°C modeled output change with power fixed at 500 MW.','For Tᵧ hold x constant: derivative of 5y is 5 and of xy is x, so Tᵧ=5+x.','At (9,5), Tᵧ=14°C per one y-unit, which is 100 MW.','Thus the change per 1 MW is 14/100=0.14°C at fixed incoming temperature 9°C. The source one-megawatt wording misses this scale factor.','For reference T(9,5)=18+25+45−40=48°C; it is a temperature value rather than a marginal change.','Because T is affine in either input when the other is fixed, these increments are exact within this stipulated model.'],'answer':'Tₓ(9,5)=7°C/°C; Tᵧ(9,5)=14°C per 100 MW = 0.14°C/MW.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':302,'jpg':'155_page_2.jpg'},{'page':303,'jpg':'156_page_1.jpg','verification':'Both model and source interpretation checked; scale error explained separately'}]}]
